@page "/foreign"

<PageTitle>Wrapping a foreign control</PageTitle>
<h1>Wrapping a foreign control</h1>
<p>The select below is "foreign" — Formidable does not wrap it. The renderless FormidableField hands its template everything needed: element id, css class, aria attributes, and change notification. This page uses <code>&lt;label for="@@field.ElementId"&gt;</code> instead of wrapping the control in a label — wrapping isn't possible because the label must target the foreign element's own id, which only the field context supplies; every other page in this sample wraps.</p>

<TeachingPanel CodeFiles="@(new[] { "ForeignControl.razor", "ForeignControl.razor.cs", "GadgetOrder.cs" })">
    <Rules>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Colour</strong> — required, validated live.</li>
            <li>The select is a control Formidable does not wrap. The renderless <code>FormidableField</code> hands its template everything a wrapper would have supplied: <code>ElementId</code>, <code>CssClass</code>, aria attributes, and <code>NotifyChanged()</code>.</li>
            <li>Compare with Quickstart: wrapped inputs label-wrap and need no id; a foreign control keeps its own element, so the label targets <code>field.ElementId</code> instead.</li>
        </ul>
    </Rules>
    <TryIt>
        <ol>
            <li>Submit empty — the select gets the same red border and message treatment as any wrapped input.</li>
            <li>Pick a colour — the state clears live.</li>
            <li>Open <em>Show the code</em> and compare this page's select wiring with Quickstart's one-line wrapped input.</li>
        </ol>
    </TryIt>
</TeachingPanel>

<FormidableForm Model="_order" OnValidSubmit="HandleValid">
    <FormidableSummary />

    <FormidableField For="() => _order.Colour" Context="field">
        <div class="field">
            <label for="@field.ElementId">Colour</label>
            <select @attributes="field.InputAttributes"
                    value="@_order.Colour" @onchange="args => OnColourChanged(args, field)">
                <option value="">Choose…</option>
                <option>Red</option>
                <option>Green</option>
                <option>Blue</option>
            </select>
        </div>
        <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _order.Colour" />
    </FormidableField>

    <div class="actions">
        <button type="submit">Submit</button>
    </div>
</FormidableForm>

<p role="status">@_status</p>
